<h2>What Matters #1: Review Recency Beats Review Count</h2>
<p>A contractor whose most recent review landed yesterday is more likely to rank well than a contractor with hundreds of reviews whose most recent one is six months old. Recency tends to outweigh raw volume.</p>
<p>This lines up with the <a href="https://whitespark.ca/blog/local-search-ranking-factors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Whitespark 2026 Local Search Ranking Factors Survey</a>, where review recency jumped from roughly #20 in importance in 2023 to a top-5 position. The consistent message from local SEO practitioners: the moment a business stops generating new reviews, its local rankings tend to start slipping — even if the total review count is massive.</p>
<h3>Why This Matters More for Contractors</h3>
<p>Contractors face a unique challenge: most customers only need their services once every 5-15 years (a roof replacement, an HVAC install). That natural cycle makes consistent review generation harder than it is for restaurants or salons with repeat weekly customers.</p>
<p>Contractors who stay ahead of this tend to do three things differently:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>They ask immediately.</strong> Sending a review request within 24 hours of job completion — while the experience is still fresh — consistently outperforms waiting.</li>
<li><strong>They ask everyone.</strong> Not just the homeowner who paid the bill — property managers, real estate agents, and insurance adjusters who interacted with the work are all fair game.</li>
<li><strong>They use multiple touchpoints.</strong> Text, email, and in-person requests all help. A short text message sent shortly after job completion is one of the highest-converting review requests a contractor can send.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Set up an automated review request system that triggers within 24 hours of job completion. Aim for a steady cadence of new reviews each month rather than a one-time push for volume — if your total review count is high but recency is poor, your visibility is still at risk.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #2: Photo Quantity Is More Important Than Most Contractors Think</h2>
<p>The SEO industry has long treated GBP photos as a "nice to have" rather than a ranking driver. Google has been cagey about whether photos are a direct ranking factor or purely an engagement signal — but the practical case for keeping a large, fresh photo library is strong enough that it belongs near the top of any contractor's GBP checklist.</p>
<h3>Why Photos Likely Help</h3>
<p>A few mechanisms are probably at work:</p>
<p><strong>1. Behavioral signals.</strong> Google has indicated that listings with more photos tend to generate meaningfully more direction requests and website clicks than listings without. Those engagement signals likely feed back into how prominently a profile surfaces.</p>
<p><strong>2. Vision AI analysis.</strong> Google uses computer vision to analyze photo content. A roofing contractor uploading photos of shingle installs, gutter work, and storm damage helps Google understand what services they actually offer — improving relevance matching for those searches.</p>
<p><strong>3. Profile completeness signal.</strong> A large, active photo library is a reasonable proxy for overall profile maintenance. Contractors who upload photos regularly are usually also responding to reviews, keeping hours current, and posting — the whole profile tends to be in better shape.</p>
<p><strong>4. Trust and click-through rate.</strong> In a local pack full of thin profiles, a business with a rich photo gallery stands out visually and tends to earn more clicks — which is itself a positive signal.</p>
<h3>What to Photograph</h3>
<p>A well-rounded contractor photo library typically includes:</p>
<ul class="bullet-list">
<li><strong>Before/after photos:</strong> Roof replacements, HVAC installs, remodels — the more dramatic the transformation, the better</li>
<li><strong>Team photos:</strong> Crews on-site, uniformed technicians, branded vehicles</li>
<li><strong>Equipment/vehicle photos:</strong> Branded trucks, specialized tools, safety gear</li>
<li><strong>Project process photos:</strong> Mid-job progress shots, detail work, cleanup</li>
<li><strong>Office/facility photos:</strong> Showroom, warehouse, dispatch center</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Consistency beats bulk.</strong> A one-time upload of dozens of photos followed by months of silence isn't nearly as effective as adding a handful of new photos every week, on an ongoing basis.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Build out a solid photo library across all the categories above, then add a few new photos every week rather than in occasional batches. Use original images — not stock photos.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #3: Primary Category Selection Is Likely Your Highest-Impact Lever</h2>
<p>Of everything on this list, primary category selection is the one thing local SEO practitioners most consistently point to as the highest-leverage fix on a Google Business Profile. It's not a numerical tweak — it's a binary question: does your primary category directly match what your ideal customer types into Google, or is it a broader, tangential category?</p>
<h3>What "Aligned" Means in Practice</h3>
