<h3>The Category Trap</h3>
<p>A common pattern among under-performing profiles: contractors select an overly broad category hoping to capture more search volume. A roofing company selects "Construction company," hoping to also rank for general construction searches. An electrician selects "Home improvement" to cast a wider net.</p>
<p>This tends to backfire. Google's algorithm leans heavily on primary category as a relevance signal. When someone searches "roofing contractor," Google favors profiles categorized specifically as "Roofing contractor" — not profiles that happen to offer roofing among a dozen other services.</p>
<h3>The Secondary Category Strategy</h3>
<p>A small number of tightly related secondary categories tends to outperform either zero secondaries or a long, scattershot list — enough to signal breadth without diluting what the profile is fundamentally about.</p>
