<p>Audit your primary category. If it doesn't exactly match what your ideal customer types into Google, change it. Then select a handful of highly relevant secondary categories — resist the temptation to add tangential ones "just in case."</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #4: Post Frequency Creates a Real Ranking Advantage</h2>
<p>GBP posts expire after 7 days. A profile that hasn't posted in a month shows nothing in the "Updates" section of its Knowledge Panel — which reads as inactivity to both Google and potential customers.</p>
<p>There's likely a deeper mechanism at work too. Posts generate direct engagement (clicks, calls, direction requests) that feed behavioral signals, and they let you reinforce keywords naturally — a roofing contractor posting about "hail damage repair in Plano" signals relevance for that specific query.</p>
<h3>What Works to Post</h3>
<p>Contractors who post consistently tend to rotate through a handful of content types:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Project updates:</strong> "Just completed a full roof replacement in Frisco. Here's the before and after."</li>
<li><strong>Seasonal/timely content:</strong> "Is your AC ready for the Phoenix summer? Schedule a tune-up before the heat hits."</li>
<li><strong>Offers and promotions:</strong> "$500 off a full HVAC system replacement. Limited time."</li>
<li><strong>Educational content:</strong> "3 signs your water heater is about to fail."</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What doesn't work:</strong> generic motivational quotes, irrelevant memes, or content that never mentions the service area.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Post at least twice per week. A simple content calendar works well: Monday (project update), Wednesday (educational tip), Friday (offer or seasonal reminder). Each post should include a photo, a location reference, and a clear call-to-action button.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #5: Service-Area Businesses Face a Structural Disadvantage (But Can Overcome It)</h2>
<p>Google's local algorithm relies heavily on proximity — Google itself names distance, alongside relevance and prominence, as one of the three core inputs to local ranking. For brick-and-mortar locations, proximity is straightforward: a pin on a map. For service-area businesses (SABs) with hidden addresses, Google has to infer proximity from a defined service area, which is inherently less precise — and that tends to work against SABs in competitive markets.</p>
<p>The Whitespark 2026 survey reflects this shift too: "address showing on GBP" rose in importance in recent survey years, and SABs have reported increased difficulty competing for top positions in crowded markets.</p>
<h3>How Strong SABs Compensate</h3>
<p>SABs that do rank well tend to share a few habits:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tightly defined service areas.</strong> Listing specific cities rather than a 100-mile radius helps Google — and searchers — understand where you actually operate.</li>
<li><strong>Location pages on their website.</strong> Dedicated pages for each major service area with unique content, not duplicated templates, reinforce geographic relevance beyond the GBP itself.</li>
<li><strong>Review location mentions.</strong> Encouraging customers to mention their city in reviews ("Great plumber in Richardson") reinforces geographic relevance.</li>
<li><strong>Local schema markup.</strong> Proper LocalBusiness schema with <code>areaServed</code> properties defined.</li>
<li><strong>Consistent local citations.</strong> Directories that support service-area listings (Yelp, Angi, BBB) should carry consistent NAP + service area data.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>If you're a service-area business, don't just hide your address and hope for the best. Define specific service areas by city, build unique location pages, encourage location-specific reviews, and expect that you'll need stronger overall signals to compete head-to-head with brick-and-mortar competitors.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #6: Business Description Length Matters Less Than You Think</h2>
<p>A common piece of GBP advice is "write a long, keyword-rich business description." In practice, there's little reason to believe longer descriptions outperform shorter ones. What actually seems to matter is whether the description clearly states your primary service and at least one target city — not how many characters it runs.</p>
<h3>What Actually Matters in Descriptions</h3>
<p><strong>Example of an effective description (142 characters):</strong></p>
<blockquote class="highlight-quote">
<p>"Licensed roofing contractor serving Dallas, Plano, and Frisco. Specializing in hail damage repair, full replacements, and emergency tarping."</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Example of an ineffective description (680 characters):</strong></p>
<blockquote class="highlight-quote">
<p>"At Smith Family Construction, we believe in quality, integrity, and putting the customer first. Founded in 1987, our family-owned business has served the community for over three decades. We take pride in every project, big or small, and our commitment to excellence has made us a trusted name in home services. Our team of dedicated professionals brings passion and expertise to every job site..."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The second description is longer but contains zero searchable keywords. It tells a story, but it doesn't help Google — or a searcher skimming results — understand what the business does or where it operates.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Write a concise description (150-300 characters) that includes your primary service, your top 2-3 service areas, and one differentiator. Skip the lengthy origin story or mission statement — the description field is for relevance signaling, not brand storytelling.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #7: Review Response Rate Correlates With Visibility</h2>
<p>Google has never confirmed review responses as a direct ranking factor, but there's a solid case for treating a 100% response rate as table stakes.</p>
<h3>Why Responses Matter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Engagement signal.</strong> Responding to every review demonstrates active profile management — a signal of business legitimacy.</li>
<li><strong>Keyword reinforcement.</strong> Responses let you naturally mention services and locations. A reply to a roofing review saying "Thanks for trusting us with your Plano roof replacement" reinforces both geographic and service relevance.</li>
<li><strong>Consumer behavior.</strong> BrightLocal's 2026 Local Consumer Review Survey found that 80% of consumers are more likely to use a business that answers every review. Higher conversion from search results feeds positive behavioral signals.</li>
<li><strong>Review velocity encouragement.</strong> When customers see a business responding to every review, they're more likely to leave one themselves — a modest virtuous cycle.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The Response Template That Works</h3>
<p>The best responses aren't generic copy-paste replies. A simple formula works well:</p>
<blockquote class="highlight-quote">
<p>"Thanks [Name]! [Specific reference to their project]. We're glad [specific outcome]. If you ever need [related service] in [area], we're here. — [Owner/Manager name], [Company]"</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
<blockquote class="highlight-quote">
<p>"Thanks Jennifer! Your hail-damaged roof in Frisco was one of the more challenging jobs we've done this season. We're glad the insurance process went smoothly and you're happy with the new shingles. If you ever need gutter work, we're here. — Mike, Dallas Roofing Pros"</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This response mentions the service (roof, hail damage, gutters), the location (Frisco, Dallas), and includes a real name — all in about 40 words.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Respond to 100% of reviews within 24-48 hours. Use the response as an opportunity to reinforce keywords naturally. Avoid generic templates like "Thanks for your review!" — that's wasted real estate.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #8: Secondary Categories — More Isn't Better</h2>
<p>A small number of closely related secondary categories tends to outperform both zero secondaries and a long list of loosely related ones.</p>
<h3>The Dilution Problem</h3>
<p>This likely reflects how Google's relevance scoring works. Each secondary category adds potential relevance for related queries, but also dilutes the primary category's signal. A "Plumber" with eight secondaries including "Landscaper" and "Painter" reads less like a focused plumbing business and more like a general handyman service.</p>
<p>Google's algorithm tends to reward specialization. A profile that clearly signals "we do this one thing exceptionally well" tends to outperform one that signals "we do a little of everything."</p>
<h3>An Exception: Restoration</h3>
<p>Restoration is one trade where a few more secondary categories can make sense. Water damage, fire damage, and mold remediation are closely related services that customers often need simultaneously, so a restoration company covering multiple damage types looks comprehensive rather than diluted.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Select a handful (2-4) of secondary categories that are closely related to your primary service, and remove anything tangential. If you're a plumber with "Painter" or "Carpenter" as secondaries, they're more likely hurting than helping.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #9: The "Openness" Signal Is Becoming a Hard Filter</h2>
<p>Google's "openness" signal — whether a business appears currently open at the moment of search — has become one of the more consequential factors for time-sensitive searches. For queries with clear urgent intent ("emergency plumber near me" at 10 PM), a business that shows as closed is far less likely to surface prominently, regardless of how strong its other signals are.</p>
<h3>The Emergency Service Opportunity</h3>
<p>Contractors offering 24/7 emergency service — plumbing, HVAC, electrical, restoration — have a real advantage here if their listed hours reflect it. If you offer after-hours service but your GBP shows 9-5 hours, you're effectively invisible for the highest-intent searches: the ones that happen at 10 PM when a pipe bursts.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>If you offer any form of after-hours service — even just emergency call-taking — update your GBP hours to reflect it. You don't need technicians on-site 24/7; you need someone answering the phone. An after-hours answering service, paired with hours that reflect it, can meaningfully improve visibility for emergency queries.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #10: Ranking Factors Vary by Trade</h2>
<p>What matters most doesn't weigh the same across every trade.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Solar</strong> tends to lean more heavily on photos than most trades. It's a high-consideration, high-ticket purchase, and visual proof of past installations does a lot of the trust-building work.</li>
<li><strong>Restoration and other emergency trades</strong> lean more heavily on review recency. When someone has a flooded basement, a two-year-old review doesn't reassure them nearly as much as one from last week.</li>
<li><strong>Lower-competition trades</strong> can sometimes rank with a lighter overall signal profile simply because the local bar is lower in a given market — though that bar tends to rise every year.</li>
<li><strong>Roofing</strong> is typically one of the most competitive contractor categories in a given market, which means strong performance is usually needed across every signal, not just one or two.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Don't apply generic local SEO advice blindly. If you're a solar contractor, prioritize photos and recent reviews above almost everything else. If you're in a lower-competition trade, don't get complacent — benchmark against your specific trade and market, not "local businesses" in general.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Matters #11: Q&amp;A Activity No Longer Matters (And the Feature Is Going Away)</h2>
<p>Despite years of SEO advice recommending "seed your Q&amp;A section with common questions," Q&amp;A activity was never confirmed by Google as a ranking signal — and the feature itself is being phased out in favor of AI-generated answers.</p>
<h3>Why Q&amp;A Never Mattered Much</h3>
<p>Q&amp;A content was never indexed in traditional search results — it appeared only on the Knowledge Panel, which relatively few users expanded. Google never confirmed it as a ranking factor, and it's increasingly being replaced by AI-generated answers powered by Gemini through Google's "Ask this place" feature in Maps.</p>
<h3>What Replaces Q&amp;A</h3>
<p>Google's "Ask this place" feature uses AI to generate answers from multiple sources:<br />
- Your website content<br />
- Your GBP description and services<br />
- Your reviews (and review responses)<br />
- Third-party mentions and citations</p>
<p>This shift makes your <strong>website FAQ content</strong> and <strong>review response keywords</strong> more important than ever. The Q&amp;A section you used to control directly is being replaced by AI-generated answers based on data you only influence indirectly.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>If you invested heavily in GBP Q&amp;A, redirect that energy to:<br />
1. Building a comprehensive FAQ page on your website with schema markup<br />
2. Writing keyword-rich review responses that mention services and locations<br />
3. Making sure your website content clearly answers common customer questions</p>
<hr />
<h2>What This Means for Your GBP Strategy</h2>
<p>Let's translate this into a prioritized action plan.</p>
<h3>Tier 1: Do These First (Highest Impact)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Audit your primary category.</strong> If it doesn't exactly match your core service, change it today.</li>
<li><strong>Implement a review generation system.</strong> Target a steady stream of new reviews per month with automated requests within 24 hours of job completion.</li>
<li><strong>Build a solid photo library</strong> across all categories, then add a few new photos weekly.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Tier 2: Do These Next (Strong Impact)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Post twice per week</strong> on your GBP with project updates, seasonal tips, and offers.</li>
<li><strong>Respond to 100% of reviews</strong> within 24-48 hours using keyword-rich, personalized replies.</li>
<li><strong>Select a handful of secondary categories</strong> — remove any tangential ones.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Tier 3: Do These When Tier 1-2 Are Complete (Moderate Impact)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Update business hours</strong> to reflect any after-hours or emergency service availability.</li>
<li><strong>Tighten your service area</strong> to specific cities rather than a broad radius.</li>
<li><strong>Write a concise business description</strong> (150-300 characters) with your primary service and top locations.</li>
<li><strong>Complete all available attributes</strong> in your GBP.</li>
</ol>
<h3>What NOT to Waste Time On</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lengthy business descriptions</strong> — 300 characters is plenty</li>
<li><strong>GBP Q&amp;A</strong> — Google is removing this feature</li>
<li><strong>Piling on secondary categories</strong> — a handful is the sweet spot</li>
<li><strong>Bulk photo uploads</strong> — consistent freshness beats one-time volume</li>
<li><strong>Keyword-stuffing your business name</strong> — this can trigger suspensions and penalties</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>A Few Honest Caveats</h2>
<p>Before you treat any of this as gospel, a few things worth keeping in mind.</p>
<h3>Correlation Is Not Causation</h3>
<p>A relationship between something like photo count and ranking position might mean photos cause better rankings. Or it might mean successful, well-resourced businesses simply upload more photos. Treat directional guidance like this as informed judgment, not proof.</p>
<div class="callout callout-warning">
<p><strong>Important caveat:</strong> Even well-documented ranking factors rarely act alone. A profile that nails every recommendation above can still be outranked by proximity, competition, or factors outside its GBP entirely.</p>
</div>
<h3>Proximity Is a Massive Confounding Variable</h3>
<p>Google's local algorithm weighs proximity heavily — often more heavily than any single factor discussed above. A perfectly optimized profile 20 miles from a searcher can still lose to a mediocre profile 2 miles away. No amount of GBP optimization fully overcomes distance.</p>
<h3>This Guide Is Written for Contractors Specifically</h3>
<p>This guide focuses on home service trades — roofing, HVAC, plumbing, electrical, solar, and restoration. The relative importance of these factors may look different for restaurants, retail, healthcare, or professional services, which have different competitive dynamics.</p>
<h3>Google's Algorithm Changes Constantly</h3>
<p>What matters today may shift by next year. The Q&amp;A deprecation is a good example of how quickly the landscape can change — a feature the industry spent years optimizing for is being phased out entirely.</p>
<h3>There's More to Local Rankings Than Your GBP</h3>
<p>This guide is scoped to your Google Business Profile. It doesn't cover website quality and on-page SEO, backlink profiles, citation consistency, Google Ads / Local Services Ads spend, historical ranking trajectory, or brand search volume — all of which also influence local pack rankings and deserve separate attention.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Your 30-Day GBP Optimization Action Plan</h2>
<p>Here's exactly what to do in the next 30 days.</p>
<h3>Week 1: Foundation Fixes</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Audit and correct your primary category</li>
<li>[ ] Select a handful of secondary categories; remove extras</li>
<li>[ ] Rewrite your business description (150-300 characters, keyword-rich)</li>
<li>[ ] Verify business hours are accurate; add after-hours if applicable</li>
<li>[ ] Tighten service area to specific cities (remove broad radius)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Week 2: Content Push</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Upload 20 photos (mix of before/after, team, equipment, projects)</li>
<li>[ ] Write and publish 2 GBP posts with photos and CTAs</li>
<li>[ ] Complete all available attributes in your GBP</li>
<li>[ ] Add products/services listings if not already present</li>
<li>[ ] Verify website link and appointment link work correctly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Week 3: Review System</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Set up automated review request (text + email) triggering 24 hours post-job</li>
<li>[ ] Respond to all existing unanswered reviews</li>
<li>[ ] Create a review response template that includes services and locations</li>
<li>[ ] Train technicians to mention review requests during job closeout</li>
<li>[ ] Add review request language to invoices and follow-up emails</li>
</ul>
<h3>Week 4: Ongoing Rhythm</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Schedule 2 GBP posts per week in your calendar</li>
<li>[ ] Set a recurring task to upload 2-3 new photos weekly</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor review velocity — target a steady stream of new reviews per month</li>
<li>[ ] Check "Ask this place" AI answers for your business; correct inaccuracies</li>
<li>[ ] Track ranking positions for your top 5 keywords weekly</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion: What to Do Next</h2>
<p>Local SEO isn't about doing everything. It's about doing the right things consistently.</p>
<p>Across contractor Google Business Profiles, a fairly clear hierarchy tends to show up: category alignment and review recency matter most. Photos and posts create competitive separation. Profile completeness is table stakes. And a few commonly recommended tactics — long descriptions, Q&amp;A seeding, piling on secondary categories — deliver little to no real benefit.</p>
<p>The contractors who win in local search generally aren't the ones with the biggest budgets. They're the ones who treat their GBP as a living asset — fresh reviews, regular posts, new photos, active engagement — rather than a one-time setup task.</p>
<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Review recency beats review count.</strong> A profile with a steady trickle of fresh reviews tends to outrank one with hundreds of stale reviews.</li>
<li><strong>Primary category alignment is likely the #1 factor.</strong> Get this wrong and everything else is uphill.</li>
<li><strong>Photos are underrated.</strong> A solid, consistently growing photo library correlates with stronger visibility.</li>
<li><strong>Post at least twice per week.</strong> The jump from "inactive" to "active" tends to matter more than posting even more once you're already active.</li>
<li><strong>SABs need stronger signals.</strong> Service-area businesses face structural disadvantages that require compensation through volume and precision elsewhere.</li>
<li><strong>A handful of secondary categories is the sweet spot.</strong> More dilutes; too few misses opportunities.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Your Next Steps</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Today:</strong> Audit your primary category and secondary categories.</li>
<li><strong>This week:</strong> Set up automated review requests and upload your first batch of photos.</li>
<li><strong>This month:</strong> Establish a posting rhythm and respond to every existing review.</li>
<li><strong>This quarter:</strong> Track your ranking positions and adjust based on what moves.</li>
</ol>
<p>The tactics above are well understood. The only variable left is execution.</p>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ Section</h2>
<h3>How many reviews do I need to rank in the top 3?</h3>
<p>There's no magic number. What matters more than total count is <strong>recency</strong> — a profile with a modest review count and a few new ones this month tends to outperform a profile with hundreds of reviews and nothing recent. Aim for a steady trickle of new reviews (roughly 4-8 per month) rather than chasing a specific total.</p>
<h3>Does hiding my address hurt my rankings?</h3>
<p>Likely, to some degree. Service-area businesses with hidden addresses tend to have a harder time cracking the top 3 than brick-and-mortar competitors with visible addresses, largely because Google's algorithm relies heavily on proximity. Hiding your address is often required for compliance if customers don't visit you — the fix is to compensate with stronger signals elsewhere: more reviews, more photos, tighter service areas, and location-specific website content.</p>
<h3>How often should I post on my Google Business Profile?</h3>
<p>Post at least <strong>twice per week</strong>. The biggest jump in visibility tends to happen between "never post" and "post regularly." Posts expire after 7 days, so a profile without recent posts looks inactive to both Google and potential customers.</p>
<h3>What's the ideal number of photos for a contractor GBP?</h3>
<p>Build toward a solid library — aim for several dozen photos covering your work, team, and equipment — then add a few new photos weekly. What seems to matter most is ongoing freshness, not just total count: a profile that uploads photos in one big batch and then goes quiet tends to underperform one that adds a handful consistently over time.</p>
<h3>Should I respond to every review, even positive ones?</h3>
<p><strong>Yes.</strong> A 100% review response rate is a strong signal of active profile management, and BrightLocal's research shows most consumers are more likely to use a business that answers every review. Responses aren't just customer service — they're keyword real estate. Mention your service and location naturally: "Thanks for trusting us with your Plano roof replacement."</p>
<h3>How many secondary categories should I add?</h3>
<p><strong>A small number — roughly 2 to 4</strong> — chosen because they're closely related to your primary service, not because you want to cast a wide net. Adding too many dilutes your primary category signal. Skip tangential offerings.</p>
<h3>Does my business description length matter for rankings?</h3>
<p><strong>Not much.</strong> There's little reason to believe a longer description outperforms a shorter one. What matters more is <strong>keyword presence</strong> — include your primary service and top 2-3 locations. Skip the lengthy origin story and keep it under 300 characters.</p>
<h3>What happens if I'm closed when someone searches?</h3>
<p>You're much less likely to surface prominently, especially for urgent "near me" searches. Google's "openness" signal increasingly behaves like a hard filter for time-sensitive queries. If you offer emergency or after-hours service, update your GBP hours to reflect it — even if it's just an answering service — otherwise you're effectively invisible to the highest-intent searches.</p>
<h3>Do Google Business Profile posts really help rankings?</h3>
<p>Indirectly, yes. Posts drive engagement (clicks, calls, direction requests) that likely feeds behavioral signals, and they keep your profile looking active and current. The key is consistency — a steady rhythm of 2+ posts per week outperforms sporadic bulk posting.</p>
<h3>Is Q&amp;A still worth optimizing on my GBP?</h3>
<p><strong>No.</strong> Google is phasing out the Q&amp;A feature entirely in favor of AI-generated answers, and it was never a confirmed ranking factor to begin with. Redirect that effort to building FAQ content on your website with schema markup and writing keyword-rich review responses instead.</p>
<h3>How do ranking factors differ by trade?</h3>
<p>Meaningfully. Solar contractors tend to lean more on strong photo libraries. Restoration and other emergency trades depend heavily on review recency. Lower-competition trades can sometimes get away with lighter signals — though that gap tends to close over time. Benchmark against your specific trade, not generic "local business" advice.</p>
<h3>What's the fastest way to improve my local rankings?</h3>
<p>If you do nothing else, do these three things this week: (1) verify your primary category exactly matches what customers search for, (2) set up automated review requests to generate a steady stream of fresh reviews, and (3) upload a solid batch of high-quality photos. These three areas tend to have the largest impact for contractors.</p>
<h3>Can I rank well without a physical storefront?</h3>
<p>Yes, but it's harder. Service-area businesses typically need stronger compensating signals — more reviews, more photos, tighter service area definitions, and location-specific website content. The SABs that rank well treat their digital presence with the same precision a brick-and-mortar business brings to its physical location.</p>
<h3>How long until I see ranking improvements from GBP optimization?</h3>
<p>Based on general local SEO experience: category changes and hour updates can show impact within <strong>1-2 weeks</strong>. Review velocity improvements typically show within <strong>4-6 weeks</strong>. Photo and post frequency changes may take <strong>6-10 weeks</strong> to fully register. Local SEO is a compounding game — consistency over months beats intensity over days.</p>
<h3>Should I include keywords in my business name on GBP?</h3>
<p><strong>Be careful.</strong> While a keyword-matching business name can help, Google actively penalizes businesses that add keywords to names that aren't part of their legal business name — this can trigger suspensions. If your legal name already includes a keyword (e.g., "Dallas Roofing Pros"), that's fine. If you're "Smith Construction" trying to become "Smith Construction Roofing Dallas," you're risking your profile.</p>
<h3>What's more important: review count or review rating?</h3>
<p><strong>Neither on its own — review recency matters most.</strong> Between the two, a solidly-rated profile (roughly 4.5-4.8 stars) with steady review volume tends to outperform a "perfect" 5.0-star profile with very few reviews. Some practitioners believe an unusually flawless rating paired with a thin review count can even look suspicious to Google's spam systems, though that's more industry intuition than confirmed fact.</p>
<h3>Do citations still matter for local rankings?</h3>
<p>They're foundational but not where the biggest wins are anymore. Perfect NAP (name, address, phone) consistency across major directories is table stakes at this point. What tends to matter more for competitive differentiation today is review velocity, photo freshness, and posting consistency. Get citations right, then move on to higher-impact activities.</p>
<h3>How do I know if my GBP is fully optimized?</h3>
<p>Use this checklist: primary category aligned (yes/no), a handful of relevant secondary categories selected (yes/no), a solid, growing photo library (yes/no), 2+ posts per week (yes/no), 100% review response rate (yes/no), business hours accurate including after-hours (yes/no), 150-300 character description with keywords (yes/no), all attributes completed (yes/no). Check every box and you'll be well ahead of most local competitors.</p>
